statement.p:
Suppose that W is a three-dimensional subspace of
\real^4 with basis:

  \vvec_1 = \fourvec1111,\hspace{24pt}
  \vvec_2 = \fourvec1322,\hspace{24pt}
  \vvec_3 = \fourvec1{-3}{-3}{-3}\text{.}

We can see that this basis is not orthogonal by noting that
\vvec_1\cdot\vvec_2 = 8.  Our goal is to create an
orthogonal basis \wvec_1, \wvec_2, and
\wvec_3 for W.

li.p:
We have \vvec_1 = \wvec_1 and \vvec_2 = \wvec_2 + 2\wvec_1. Therefore, a linear combination of \vvec_1 and \vvec_1 can be rewritten as
c_1\vvec_1+c_2\vvec_2 = c_1\wvec_1+c_2(\wvec_2+2\wvec_1) = (c_1+2c_2)\wvec_1 + c_2\wvec_2\text{.}
In the same way, \wvec_1=\vvec_1 and \wvec_2 = \vvec_2-2\vvec_1 so any linear combination of \wvec_1 and \wvec_2 can be rewritten as a linear combination of \vvec_1 and \vvec_1.
